Our delta on top of vLLM PR #46639: pin the Marlin m-block to 16 instead of 64
under VLLM_BATCH_INVARIANT.

Apply AFTER pr46639.diff. Output is bitwise identical to the PR's 64 -- block_size_m
tiles the TOKEN dimension, while each row's K-reduction order is set by BLOCK_K, so
per-row arithmetic is unchanged. It is faster because 16 exactly matches one Marlin
m-block (thread_m_blocks = div_ceil(16, 16) = 1), so decode stops padding ~92 real
token-expert rows out to ~4,200 MMA rows.

Measured on 100 long documents: 1x100 334s vs 345s, 10x10 508s vs 598s (1.18x).

Do NOT use 8: div_ceil(8, 16) is also 1, so it selects the SAME kernel and merely
creates half-empty blocks. 32 (thread_m_blocks = 2) is the only other sensible value.

--- a/vllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/experts/marlin_moe.py
+++ b/vllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/experts/marlin_moe.py
@@ -320,7 +320,7 @@

     if envs.VLLM_BATCH_INVARIANT:
         # Pin block_size_m so token alignment is independent of M.
-        block_size_m = 64
+        block_size_m = 16
     else:
         # M block size selection logic
         # TODO: tune this further for specific models
@@ -491,7 +491,7 @@

     if envs.VLLM_BATCH_INVARIANT:
         # Pin block_size_m so token alignment is independent of M.
-        block_size_m = 64
+        block_size_m = 16
     else:
         # Tune block_size_m based on expert capacity to reduce padding overhead.
         block_size_m = 64
